  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Cyber GhostVPN for supporting \nPBS. Does quantum mechanics allow the future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to retroactively influence the past, as in \nthe infamous delayed choice quantum eraser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment? Well, how about we get an actual \nquantum physicist who many of you already know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16320,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to show us how to do this experiment at home and \nhopefully put the matter to rest once and for all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, a couple of announcements before we \nget started. We wanted to let you know that your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34800,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "help with liking and commenting recent episodes is \nworking. Thanks to that, the algorithm is sharing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current episodes with more of our community. \nSo, please keep it up. And if you haven't yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe and hit the bell so you don't miss \nnew episodes. We also want to let you know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over at the merch store, we have our first ever \nbackpack. It's the black hole bag. It's 28 L,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made from recycled polyester for durability, and \nguaranteed not to hawking radiate your quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information. There will be 10% off for the next \n48 hours at the merch store. Links for everything  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the description. Richard Feineman said of the \ndouble slit experiment that it has in it the heart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics in reality it contains \nthe only mystery. Well, the mystery he was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talking about is superp position. In the double \nslit experiment that means a superp position of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92240,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single particle traveling through both of the \ndouble slits at once. The experiment hints that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prior to detection at a screen, a quantum \nparticle plays out all possible realities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that may lead to that one measurement result. \nAnd the shadow of these overlapping realities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these interacting trajectories, is cast on the \nscreen in the form of an interference pattern.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117440,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you prune the reality tree early, say by \nmeasurement at one of the slits, the final pattern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of particle locations reflects that. As if the \nstandard double slit experiment wasn't strange  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough, then we have the delayed choice quantum \neraser experiments in which the measurement of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138320,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles path to the screen is made after the \npattern is recorded on the screen. The most famous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146800,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example is the one by Kim Yu Kulik Shei and Scully \nin 1999. Such experiments have been interpreted as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156480,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "saying that a future measurement can influence a \npast measurement retrocausal influence. We did an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode on the delayed choice quantum arrays a \nlong time ago. Now, we didn't quite promote the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retrocausal interpretation, but we certainly \nfailed to give any alternative. Well, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178720,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are now some really solid counter explanations to \nthe idea of retrocausality. A few papers have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "published which get at aspects of it and various \nYouTube videos also explain some of the errors in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the retrocausal interpretation. So, it's time for \nus to weigh in. Today we're actually going to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198720,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a real experiment. And by we, I mean Mithina from \nLooking Glass Universe, who's figured out a way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do the quantum eraser in a homemade setup. She's \ngoing to show you how this experiment gives the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illusion of information traveling backwards \nin time. I'm really excited to show you this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment because 12 years ago I did a video \non the delayed choice quantum eraser where I got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it completely wrong. And it was only doing this \nexperiment that made it finally click for me. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "delay choice quantum eraser is just a variation \non the usual double slit experiment. So for that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment, all you'll need is some light and two \nvery narrow beams that you can shine that laser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at. What do we expect to see? Well, it depends on \nhow we think about light. On the one hand, we know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light is a wave in the electromagnetic field. \nAnd like any wave, it should produce interference  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns as components of the wave passing through \nthe different slits overlap, stacking up or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "canceling out. At the wall, we get the classic \ndouble slit interference pattern. On the other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hand, we also think of light as particles, which \nis fair because the classical electromagnetic wave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is made up of indivisible quantum particles that \nwe call photons. An intuitive but naive picture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for particles is as a bunch of tiny balls, spray \nballs at our pair of slits, and they should end  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up forming two piles corresponding to passage \nthrough each slit. Photons are really some weird  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in between thing between waves and particles. If \nyou fire individual photons, they do look like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they make single hits on the wall as though \nthey were tiny balls. But after many firings,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the double slit pattern becomes visible. The \ninterpretation is that each photon really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passes through both slits as a wave and then \nthe final destination of the particle-like spot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is determined by the interference pattern \nmade by that wave. In quantum mechanics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we say that the position wave function of the \nphoton passes through both slits or that it exists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a superp position of both trajectories and in \nfact of all possible trajectories that reach the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wall. But when the wall measures the photon \nlocation, that superp position collapses and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single position is chosen. Even then, the path \nthe photon took to get there remains undefined.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345120,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to know the path taken, you have \nto measure it before the wall. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could place a little detector at the slits \nthat clocks the passing photon without actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stopping it. Essentially, we're collapsing \nthe position wave function of the slits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not at the wall. Now that we know which slit the \nphoton passed through, everything we see after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that has to be consistent with it only passing \nthrough that slit, which means no double slit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference pattern. One common misconception \nis that if a photon passes through just one slit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it lands ultimately just like a classical particle \nin a single pile of points. But actually, single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slit interference looks like this. It looks a lot \nlike double slit interference, but if you compare  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them side by side, you can see the difference. \nSingle slit interference still has dark fringes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but just not as many as double slit. This is a \nform of frownhoffer defraction with the edges  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the slits causing the interference in this \ncase. So photons keep behaving like waves even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we measure which slit they traversed. If you want \nto understand single slit defraction better, Mina  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did a video explaining why this is and what it \nmeans. The transition from double to single slit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference occurs when we measure which slit \nthe photon passed through. We sometimes call this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a whichway measurement. That's weird enough, but \nat least in this case, we have a sensible causal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ordering. You make the measurement, it collapses \nthe wave function, stopping it from forming a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double slit pattern further down the line. Things \nget a lot weirder in the case of the delayed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choice quantum eraser. In the classic experiment \nby Kim and collaborators, the photons passing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the slits are actually cloned using a beta \nbarium borate or BBO crystal which converts each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incoming photon into a pair of near identical \noutgoing photons that are quantum entangled.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key is that both photons in this pair carry \nthe which way information. They both know which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468000,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slit their progenitor photon passed through before \nthe BBO crystal duplicated it. So now we send one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the new pair, call it photon A, to our usual \ndouble slit detector to be observed by Alice. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other photon B goes to Bob who can extract the \nwhichway information or alternatively can choose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to erase that info. The device is simple enough. \nThe path of photon B depends on which slit it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "came through. So we can place a detector on both \npaths and see which one is triggered. We can also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choose to scramble the whichway information by \nrecombining those two paths. So there's no way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to tell them apart anymore. The bizarre result of \nthe Kim paper and similar experiments is that if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514000,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the whichway information is measured from photon \nB, then photon A acts as though we also measured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522000,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which slit it came from. Alice sees that those \nphoton A's have their whichway info measured and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fall according to a single slit pattern. But if \nthe whichway information in photon B is scrambled,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice sees the corresponding photon A's landing \naccording to the classic double slit pattern. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acts as though it did pass through both slits. \nIt's like we change photon A's behavior without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually touching it, only by measuring its \ntwin. And if that wasn't weird enough, in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kim experiment, the choice of whether to keep or \ndiscard the whichway information in photon B is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562080,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made after its entangled partner photon A reached \nits detector. That makes it seem like photon A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "landed according to a choice that hasn't been made \nyet. And this has been referred to as retrocausal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence, and it's what makes the delayed choice \nquantum eraser so befuddling. To explain how we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can get around this paradox, I'm going to do an \nanalogous experiment that you can do very easily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at home. And when I say it's analogous, what \nI mean is that it's mathematically equivalent.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like if you looked at the maths of the original \nexperiment versus this experiment, it's exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same except for how you label a few things. \nThat's not to say though that this experiment is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as cool as the other one, because the other one \ninvolves actual entanglement, whereas this uses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little trick to kind of mimic entanglement \nwithout actually making an entangled photon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which I wish I was able to do, but BBO crystals \nare very expensive. So, we've got our laser here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we've also got a double slit. In the original \nexperiment, for every photon A that goes through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the slits, they make a photon B which knows the \nslit that photon A took. So photon B is what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stores the information about which way photon A \nwent. So we need in our experiment some other way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to store that information. Well, we're going to \ndo it in a really simple way. Okay, so here's our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double slit and we've put some filters in front of \nit. two different filters in front of each of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slits. In front of slit one, we have a horizontal \npolarization filter. And in front of slit two,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have a vertical polarization filter. Well, now \nwe know that any light that makes it through this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double slit is marked. If it's horizontally \npolarized, it must have come through slit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one. Whereas, if it's vertically polarized, it \nmust have come through slit two. In other words,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the polarization is what's now marking whether \nthe light went through slit one or slit two,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is convenient for us because we can use this \ncrystal to split the light accordingly. So, this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695920,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is called calite. It's not as cool as BBO, which \nis what they used in the original experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it does have one very nice property. If I \nput it here at this angle, it splits the light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into two. These two bits of light represent the \ntwo slits of the double slit experiment. If the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715840,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light is vertical, it came from slit one. Whereas, \nif it's horizontal, it came from slit two. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light in the top pattern only went through slit \none. And the light from the bottom pattern only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "went through slit two. So, we'd expect that each \nof these patterns are going to be single slit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns. And if you look closely at them, you \ncan see that they are a double slit pattern would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a lot more dark fringes in it. So the calsite \noriented this way is acting just like Bob's which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747920,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way measurement and collapsing the light into a \nsingle slit. But if we rotate the calsite by 45°,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'll see why this new measurement is now \ngoing to look like Bob's arasia measurement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we're going to get the double slit pattern \nback. Okay, so that took me a while, but I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "managed to rotate this by 45°. And I haven't yet \nput the double slit in there, but I just want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show you what rotating the calite actually does. \nSo, if you look over there, you can see that there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778080,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are two dots. Well, there are roughly two dots. \nAgain, it's a little bit noisy because my cow site  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here is nowhere near perfect, but roughly it's two \ndots. And we're going to give the two dots names.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we're going to call the top one plus and \nwe're going to call the bottom one minus. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's annoying, but it's an inevitability of this \ngeometry that they're not aligned with each other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anymore. So, they're not one on top of each other. \nSo, with the magic of post-prouction, we'll stack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808240,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these so that they are one on top of each other. \nThat's going to make everything a little bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easier in a second. To figure out why calsite \noriented like this is going to be an erasia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement. Let's see what happens when we put \nin vertically oriented light into this calite. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825760,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can see that the total amount of light decreases \nbut both dots are still there. It's not like last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time where horizontal light all goes to the same \ndot and vertical light all goes to the other dot.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this plus and minus measurement, half of \nthe vertical light will go to plus and half will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go to minus. And same for horizontal. But then \nwhat does that tell us about light that's going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to go through this marked double slit? So light \nthat goes through the first slit is horizontally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "polarized whereas light that goes through \nthe second slit is vertically polarized. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previously our calite was letting us measure the \npolarization and therefore measure whether it went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868080,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through slit one or slit two. But now think about \nwhat this plus minus capsite is doing. It's going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take light from the first slit and it's going \nto have an equal probability of ending up at plus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or at minus. And same for the second slit. So now \njust looking at the light after it goes through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the calite isn't enough to tell us anything at \nall about which way the light went. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it scramles up the light from both paths. So it \nbasically erases all of that information that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902160,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had gotten by marking the paths. So what do we \nexpect to happen then? Well, let's find out. Oh  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910400,
    "dDurationMs": 9840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "man, that looks so good. Okay. Um, that kind of \nworked out better than I expected. Um, okay. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920240,
    "dDurationMs": 9760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a look and hopefully you can see two double \nslit patterns. So, if we use the calite in its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previous orientation, then we're using it \nto measure which way the light went and that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapses the light into going into single slit \ninterference. But if we use the calite in this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orientation, the plus minus orientation, we're \nusing it as an eraser. It erases the information  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about which slit the light went through. And so \nwe're back to the double slit. If you look really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closely at these two double slits, you'll see \nthat they don't exactly line up. And in fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they seem to be offset from each other the exact \nright amount that if you superimpose them, it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks like the single slit interference pattern. \nSo what's going on? Well, it turns out that this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the solution to the entire paradox. Let's go \nback to the original setup to see why. In the Kim  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment, photons are cloned by the BBO crystal \nand the whichway analysis is done with only one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those. We called it photon B. Just as our \nexperiment sorts the photons into two interference  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995200,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns, the original experiment sorts the B \nphotons into two bins. For the measure case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those bins tell you which slit the corresponding \nphoton A also went through. For the eraser case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are still two bins, but there is no whichway \ninformation in how photons are sorted into them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice never sees a double slit interference \npattern until something very specific occurs.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first, she sees just a blob of positions for \nall photon A's. Then Bob comes and tells her which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027840,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those had a twin photon B for which the way \ninfo was measured and for which it was erased.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The measured cases are still a blob. Basically a \nblurry single slit interference. Now, the sum of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041600,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the cases in which the whichway info was \nerased are actually a very similar mess, a blob.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050000,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, Bob also needs to tell Alice which of those \nerased photon B's ended up in each of the separate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eraser detectors. Only then can Alice disentangle \nthe featureless blob of photon A positions and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see interference bands. and her patterns look \na lot like masers, clear peaks and valleys that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072320,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are slightly offset. And when you merge them, you \nget a featureless pile. This is a critical point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079360,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's often glossed over in descriptions of the \nquantum eraser. So to recap, the solution is Alice  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always sees the single slip pattern, and that's \nregardless of what Bob chooses to do on his side.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a single slip pattern is really just two \ndouble slip patterns on top of each other. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus version and the minus version that's slightly \noffset. Now, if Bob does happen to do the eraser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement, then he and Alice can reconvene \nafterwards and sort Alice's photons into the plus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and minus groups and they find those two double \nslit patterns. In our experiment, we did this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sorting via calite, but in the real version of \nthe experiment, it just looks like post-processing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the data to make the single slit interference \npattern into two double slit ones. So instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob's arasia measurement instantly uncolapsing \nthe wave function and turning the single slit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference into the double slit, you only see \nthis effect of his measurement much later once he  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Alice sift through the data. The story told \nby the retrocausal interpretation of the delayed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choice quantum araser is that Bob's choice of \nmeasurement retroactively influences where Alice's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon lands. But the same logic works the other \nway around. The position of photon A measured by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162720,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice absolutely influences where photon B can go, \nwhere it's measured by Bob. Just for reference,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I've drawn the plus double slit pattern here and \nthe minus one here just so that we can kind of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177840,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see where they are. And then let's say that Alice \nmeasures her photon and it happens to land right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186640,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here. This lines up perfectly with the minus \ndouble slip pattern, but not at all with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus one. Now after that Bob measures his photon \nB and he chooses to do an erasia measurement. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that means that there are two possible outcomes. \nHe could get a plus or he could get a minus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1209680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But could he get a plus? I mean that wouldn't be \nconsistent with the result that Alice has already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1216080,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "got. And so actually Alice's result forces Bob's \nresult to also be minus. I should add that Alice's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement of the position of photon A only \naffects one thing for Bob, and that's which of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1230400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eraser detectors Photon B will land in, assuming \nPhoton B makes it all the way to the eraser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236640,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "section. But then one of the eraser detectors \nwill strongly favor photons whose twin landed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1244880,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one set of bands on Alice's screen, while the \nother favors the complimentary set of bands. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've had this causality thing the wrong way \naround this whole time. We thought that it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1257520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob's measurement that forced Alice's photon \nto act in a particular way. But now we can see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's the other way around. If Alice's measurement \nhappens first, then her outcome is what determines  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1271040,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what happens when Bob does his arasia measurement. \nIn other words, Alice's outcome is what forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1278400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bob's to happen in the way it does. But that's \nonly the case if Alice's measurement happens first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285120,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then Bob's. But here's the twist. Does Alice's \nmeasurement always happen first? Or does that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1293760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depend on your frame of reference? It's possible \nto set up this experiment so that Bob's detections  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1300560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually happen before Alice's just by changing \nthe path lengths for each section. If we do that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1308000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we expect exactly the same result. The same \ninterference fringes are revealed when photon A is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1314480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filtered according to where photon B lands in the \neraser. In that case, it seems that B caused A,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1321840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but with the same result as when A caused B. This \nmakes it harder to assign a causal power to either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1328560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case. It's also hard to even imagine a causal \npath connecting Alice and Bob's observations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1334880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond the sort of spooky action at a distance \nthat Einstein so hated. That said, there is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1342160,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way to explain the causation that connects Alice's \nand Bob's observations that doesn't need to assign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1349120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a causal direction. It all depends on what \nmysterious property really determines how our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1354240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons get sorted into the two detectors in the \nquantum erasor or how they are sorted into the two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1360960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference patterns by the calcite crystal in \nMithan's experiment. What property is that? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're going to have to wait for an upcoming \nepisode for that one. But in the meantime,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1373280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go check out Mith's channel for quantum deep \ndives and more DIY quantum experiments. It's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1380080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looking glass universe, and it's amazing. I also \nhad a long and really fun chat with Mna about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1386800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mysteries, her work, and a lot more. It's \nin our new series of space-time conversations that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1393040,
